This is the classic AC'97 controller. BAR0 (r0=0xc000) is the NAM (mixer) register set, BAR1 (r1=0xc400) is the NABM (bus master) register set. 

Let me set up my plan. The AC'97 approach:
- NAM (BAR0): reset (0x00), set master volume (0x02), PCM out volume (0x18)
- NABM (BAR1): PCM Out box at offset 0x10. BDBAR (0x10), LVI (0x15), CR (0x1B). Build a Buffer Descriptor List in DMA, fill PCM samples, point at it, set last-valid-index, start.

AC'97 fixed rate is 48000 Hz. Let me write the PCM buffer and descriptors, then start playback.
[plan_set steps=5 goal=AC'97 driver plays an audible tone through pci00:04.0 -> 5]
Let me first bring up the mixer and verify it responds, before committing to filling the whole buffer. I'll reset the mixer, read back the vendor ID from NAM to confirm the codec is alive, and set volumes.

I need a sine table without floating point. I'll generate a square-ish/triangle tone using integer math instead — actually a simple approach: produce a tone by writing a piecewise waveform. Let me generate an approximate sine using a small precomputed quarter-wave via integer stepping. Simpler and clearly audible: a triangle wave computed with integer ramps.

Let me start with the mixer bring-up and codec check.
